-- title:
--   (iii″) — for K₂ = Q, condition (iii) says the columns of Y and the first-column differences lie in K
-- statement:
--   Let $K \subseteq Q$ be a closed convex cone in $\mathbb R^{n+1}$, and let $Y$ be an $(n+1)\times(n+1)$ real matrix that is symmetric and satisfies $y_{ii} = y_{0i}$ for $1 \le i \le n$ (conditions (i) and (ii)). Write $Y e_j$ for the $j$th column of $Y$ ($0 \le j \le n$). Then $Y \in M(K, Q)$ if and only if
--   $$Y e_j \in K \ \ (0 \le j \le n) \qquad\text{and}\qquad Y e_0 - Y e_i \in K \ \ (1 \le i \le n).$$
--   In the paper's words: "In this case, $K_2^* = Q^*$ is generated by the vectors $e_i$ and $f_i$ [where $f_i = e_0 - e_i$], and hence (iii′) has the following convenient form: (iii″) Every column of $Y$ is in $K$; the difference of the first column and any other column is in $K$."
--
--   This column description of $M(K)$ is the working form of condition (iii) used in the proofs of Lemmas 1.3 and 1.5.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Coordinates are indexed by `Option ι`, with `none` the 0th coordinate; the "first column" is column `none`. The paper derives (iii″) from the rewriting (iii′) $YK_2^* \subseteq K_1$, which uses $K^{**} = K$; the paper tacitly takes $K$ closed (polyhedral in all its applications), and the hypothesis `IsClosed K` is added for this reason.
-- source:
--   Lovász and Schrijver, Cones of matrices and set-functions and 0–1 optimization, SIAM J. Optim. 1(2) (1991), p. 170, Section 1.a, (iii″)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LovaszSchrijver_IntegerHull_Basic
import Definitions.Def_LovaszSchrijver_IntegerHull_MatrixCone
open Matrix Pointwise

namespace LovaszSchrijver.IntegerHull

theorem mem_M_Q_iff_columns {ι : Type} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι]
    (K : Set (Option ι → ℝ)) (hK : IsConvexCone K) (hKc : IsClosed K) (hKQ : K ⊆ Q)
    (Y : Matrix (Option ι) (Option ι) ℝ) (hY_symm : Y.IsSymm)
    (hY_diag : ∀ i : ι, Y (some i) (some i) = Y none (some i)) :
    Y ∈ M K Q ↔
      (∀ j : Option ι, Y *ᵥ Pi.single j 1 ∈ K) ∧
        ∀ i : ι, Y *ᵥ Pi.single none 1 - Y *ᵥ Pi.single (some i) 1 ∈ K := by sorry

end LovaszSchrijver.IntegerHull
